  "body": {
    "id": "thm:score-vector-minimax-rate",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "In the canonical score-vector experiment, suppose \\(e_1\\ne e_0\\), \\(p=1/2\\), and \\(\\alpha+\\beta<1\\).  For every fixed \\(a\\) satisfying \\(0<a\\le2B\\) and every integer \\(n>4r+1\\),\n\\[\n \\frac{a^2(1-\\alpha-\\beta)}{\\sqrt{2N_n}}\n \\le s_{n,\\alpha,\\beta}^{\\mathrm{score}}\n \\le \\rho_n(\\alpha,\\beta).\n\\]\nConsequently, for fixed \\(r,B,\\varepsilon,\\alpha,\\beta\\) with \\(\\alpha+\\beta<1\\) and \\(p=1/2\\),\n\\[\n s_{n,\\alpha,\\beta}^{\\mathrm{score}}=\\Theta(n^{-1/2}).\n\\]\nThe upper bound is witnessed by the unchanged sample-mean-centered test \\(\\phi_{n,\\alpha}\\).  The exactly unbiased statistic \\(\\widehat\\Omega_n^{\\mathrm u}\\) additionally gives a common score-vector test with level at most \\(\\alpha\\), power at least \\(1-\\beta\\) at separation \\(q_n^{\\mathrm u}(\\alpha)+q_n^{\\mathrm u}(\\beta)=O(n^{-1/2})\\), and no sample-centering bias term.  The score-vector lower bound is a corollary of the stronger lower bound for common Borel tests of \\(O_n^{\\mathrm{obs}}\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "\\(p\\)",
      "\\(\\alpha\\)",
      "\\(\\beta\\)",
      "\\(B\\)",
      "\\(\\varepsilon\\)",
      "\\(r\\)",
      "\\(e_1\\)",
      "\\(e_0\\)",
      "\\(n\\)",
      "\\(N_n\\)",
      "\\(\\mathcal M_{2,\\mathrm{loc}}\\)",
      "\\(\\Omega_{\\mathrm{pop}}\\)",
      "\\(O_n^{\\mathrm{obs}}\\)",
      "\\(W_n\\)",
      "\\(s_{n,\\alpha,\\beta}^{\\mathrm{score}}\\)",
      "\\(\\rho_n(\\alpha,\\beta)\\)",
      "\\(\\phi_{n,\\alpha}\\)",
      "\\(\\widehat\\Omega_n^{12}\\)",
      "\\(\\widehat\\Omega_n^{\\mathrm u}\\)",
      "\\(q_n^{\\mathrm u}(\\delta)\\)"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:minimax-handle",
      "thm:full-observable-minimax-rate",
      "prop:exact-unbiased-lagged-statistic"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "The minimax-handle definition supplies a fixed Borel map \\(T_n\\) such that\n\\(W_n=T_n(O_n^{\\mathrm{obs}})\\).  Every score test \\(\\psi_n\\in\\Psi_n\\) therefore induces the full-observation test \\(\\psi_n\\circ T_n\\in\\Phi_n^{\\mathrm{obs}}\\), with the same rejection probability under every \\(P\\in\\mathcal M_{2,\\mathrm{loc}}\\).  Thus the score-test class is a subclass of the full-observation test class, and the two minimax definitions imply\n\\[\n s_{n,\\alpha,\\beta}^{\\mathrm{score}}\n \\ge s_{n,\\alpha,\\beta}^{\\mathrm{obs}}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\nThe full-observable minimax theorem consequently gives, for every \\(0<a\\le2B\\),\n\\[\n s_{n,\\alpha,\\beta}^{\\mathrm{score}}\n \\ge\\frac{a^2(1-\\alpha-\\beta)}{\\sqrt{2N_n}}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\nFor the upper bound, \\(\\phi_{n,\\alpha}\\) is itself a common Borel function of the score vector \\(W_n\\).  The uniform size-and-separation theorem therefore gives\n\\[\n s_{n,\\alpha,\\beta}^{\\mathrm{score}}\n \\le\\rho_n(\\alpha,\\beta). \\tag{3}\n\\]\nThe exactly unbiased proposition separately shows that\n\\(\\mathbf 1\\{\\widehat\\Omega_n^{\\mathrm u}>q_n^{\\mathrm u}(\\alpha)\\}\\)\nis a common Borel score-vector test with level at most \\(\\alpha\\), power at least \\(1-\\beta\\) whenever\n\\[\n \\Omega_{\\mathrm{pop}}\n \\ge q_n^{\\mathrm u}(\\alpha)+q_n^{\\mathrm u}(\\beta),\n\\]\nand no sample-centering bias allowance.  Finally choose any fixed \\(a\\in(0,2B]\\).  Since \\(N_n=n-2r\\), the right side of (2) is a positive constant times \\(n^{-1/2}\\), whereas (3) and the established formula for \\(\\rho_n\\) give an \\(O(n^{-1/2})\\) upper bound for fixed \\(r,B,\\varepsilon,\\alpha,\\beta\\).  Hence\n\\[\n s_{n,\\alpha,\\beta}^{\\mathrm{score}}=\\Theta(n^{-1/2}).\n\\]\nThis conclusion concerns common Borel tests of \\(W_n\\); inequality (1) transfers the stronger full-observation converse to that restricted experiment.",
    "justification": "The score-vector result is retained as an operational corollary of the stronger full-observable minimax theorem.",
    "gap": "The earlier four-atom score construction did not survive observation of raw outcomes; the new four-cell schedule closes the full-data likelihood comparison before restriction to scores.",
    "consumer": "Implementations that retain only the two IPW score arrays inherit the same sharp root-sample-size separation rate.",
